body:
This doesn't look right. I'm not sure why it isn't symmetrical, one uses the selection from line but the other just uses the caret line.

For instance it looks like if you drag the end before the start, or if you make a selection with the caret before the selection origin and try to use the start handle, it won't work right.

The way `SELECTION_HANDLE_START/END` currently work is they correspond to the selection from/to respectively. But when you drag one it needs to be able to swap to the other handle if it is moved to the other side of the other handle.
For dragging, it's easier to handle knowing if you are dragging the selection origin or the caret rather than the selection from/to. 

So, the `selection_handle_drag_type` should be set based on if its the caret or the origin of the selection (and the enum can be changed):
```cpp
				if (touch->get_position().distance_to(start_pos + Vector2(0, line_height + selection_handle_radius)) < selection_handle_radius * 2) {
					selection_handle_drag_type = is_caret_after_selection_origin(c) ? SELECTION_HANDLE_ORIGIN : SELECTION_HANDLE_CARET;
					dragging_caret_index = c;
					_cancel_inertial_scroll();
					accept_event();
					return; // Return early to block default touch logic
				}
				if (touch->get_position().distance_to(end_pos + Vector2(0, line_height + selection_handle_radius)) < selection_handle_radius * 2) {
					selection_handle_drag_type = is_caret_after_selection_origin(c) ? SELECTION_HANDLE_CARET : SELECTION_HANDLE_ORIGIN;
```

and then this code can be:
```suggestion
			if (selection_handle_drag_type == SELECTION_HANDLE_CARET ) {
				select(get_selection_origin_line(c), get_selection_origin_column(c), pos.y, pos.x, c);
			else {
				select(pos.y, pos.x, get_caret_line(c), get_caret_column(c), c);
			}
```

