body:
Handles can overlap when the selection is small, so instead of checking the start handle first and then the end handle, it would be better to get the closest one instead.

<img width="60" height="72" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2f8f7a84-b429-4b7f-84a0-fc27edfd1c14" />

The amount they overlap in this image looks like touching in the center of the end handle would grab the start handle. Something like:

```suggestion
				float start_dist = touch->get_position().distance_to(start_pos + Vector2(0, line_height + selection_handle_radius));
				bool touch_start = start_pos.x != -1 && start_dist < selection_handle_radius * 2;
				float end_dist = touch->get_position().distance_to(end_pos + Vector2(0, line_height + selection_handle_radius));
				bool touch_end = end_pos.x != -1 && end_dist < selection_handle_radius * 2;
				if (!touch_start && !touch_end) {
					continue;
				}
				if (touch_start && touch end) {
					touch_start = start_dist < end_dist;
					touch_end = !touch_start;
				}
				if (touch_start) {
					selection_handle_drag_type = is_caret_after_selection_origin(c) ? SELECTION_HANDLE_START : SELECTION_HANDLE_END;
					selection_handle_drag_offset = touch->get_position() - Vector2(start_pos);
				} else {
					selection_handle_drag_type = is_caret_after_selection_origin(c) ? SELECTION_HANDLE_END : SELECTION_HANDLE_START;
					selection_handle_drag_offset = touch->get_position() - Vector2(end_pos);
				}
				dragging_caret_index = c;
				_cancel_inertial_scroll();
				accept_event();
				return; // Return early to block default touch logic
```

